<p>Pakistan and China emerged as key outside players in the efforts that helped secure a temporary two-week ceasefire between the United States and Iran, according to the collected reports. Islamabad facilitated contacts, while Beijing pressed Tehran toward accepting the deal. The reporting says Washington and Islamabad believed Iran would be more likely to consider a US-backed proposal if it came through Pakistan, a Muslim-majority neighbor that had publicly emphasized neutrality during the conflict. Pakistani officials described their role as one of facilitation rather than authorship, while Pakistani and Iranian sources cited by media outlets said Chinese assurances and calls for restraint helped create space for diplomacy. The diplomacy was still contested even as it produced a pause in fighting. Reuters-based reporting said Pakistan launched an overnight push when mediation was close to collapse, and Washington was preparing to send a high-level negotiating team led by Vice President J.D. Vance to Islamabad for formal talks on ending hostilities after the truce announcement. At the same time, Israel&#x27;s envoy questioned Pakistan&#x27;s credibility as a mediator, underscoring that while the truce has opened a hopeful path to direct US-Iran talks, it has not erased the mistrust among the countries involved.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/7BJURXHPL2o9aOUihyQcVvmui030pVm8vi-qDgUHjFDUkM0F0SHpJoDpx2so5WB3UgZBBF0uEW6C0E6mWxp3P3NauAmhK08NLFpCWo8x3_Vb' alt='Pakistan’s Ambassador to the US Rizwan Saeed Sheikh speaks during a television interview as Islamabad describes its role in facilitating the US-Iran ceasefire.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Bleak mood: The Guardian reported that at about 5pm on Tuesday, Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif told his cabinet to brace for the war&#x27;s impact and said the chances for peace had grown dim, underscoring how close Islamabad believed the region was to a broader crisis.</li><li>Assurance mattered: Dawn reported that Iran&#x27;s initial conditions for substantial talks included outside assurances that a ceasefire would hold, which helps explain why Chinese reassurance was seen as important.</li><li>Beijing&#x27;s motive: The New York Times said China&#x27;s push reflected both its growing influence with Tehran and its desire to avoid a wider war that could destabilize the region.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Pakistan government: Pakistani officials cast their country as a neutral facilitator trusted by all sides, with Ambassador Rizwan Saeed Sheikh saying the breakthrough grew out of weeks of discreet consultations. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1990131/pakistans-envoy-to-us-acknowledges-chinas-quiet-but-persuasive-role-in-iran-talks'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>Israel: Israel&#x27;s envoy challenged Pakistan&#x27;s role, saying it was not a credible intermediary and tying the approach to earlier Trump-team reliance on Qatar and Turkey in Gaza negotiations. (<a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-iran-war-news-not-credible-player-israel-envoy-on-paks-mediator-role-in-us-iran-talks-11331793#publisher=newsstand'>NDTV</a>)</li><li>China: Iranian officials told The New York Times that Beijing pressed Tehran to accept the ceasefire, showing China&#x27;s willingness to use its influence to prevent a wider conflict. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/08/world/asia/china-iran-cease-fire.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Pakistani analysts: Analysts cited by The Guardian said Islamabad turned fears of regional spillover into a diplomatic opening, giving Pakistan a rare foreign-policy success after intense behind-the-scenes efforts. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/08/pakistan-us-israel-iran-ceasefire'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ndtv.com/world-news/us-iran-news-did-us-push-peacemaker-pakistan-for-iran-ceasefire-what-report-said-11332169#publisher=newsstand'>US Was Desperate For Iran Ceasefire, Pushed Pakistan For A Deal: Report</a> - ndtv.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/08/pakistan-us-israel-iran-ceasefire'>How Pakistan secured ‘biggest diplomatic win in years’ with Iran ceasefire</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/talks-were-almost-dead-pakistans-last-ditch-effort-to-secure-iran-war-truce'>‘Talks were almost dead’: Pakistan’s last-ditch effort to secure Iran war truce</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/08/world/asia/china-iran-cease-fire.html'>China Pressed Iran Toward Cease-Fire, Iranian Officials Say</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.semafor.com/article/04/08/2026/china-reportedly-pressed-iran-to-accept-truce'>China reportedly pressed Iran to accept truce</a> - semafor.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1990131/pakistans-envoy-to-us-acknowledges-chinas-quiet-but-persuasive-role-in-iran-talks'>Pakistan’s envoy to US acknowledges China’s quiet but persuasive role in Iran talks</a> - dawn.com</li></ul>